@node	0.1, 0.2, Chapter 0, Chapter 0
@section Running this book

This section covers the pinned toolchain, the @command{just} recipes, the
layout of @file{examples/}, @file{exercises/}, and @file{solutions/}, and how
a test proves an exercise. Running the code is optional: every result a
listing displays is also written in the listing as a comment, so the book
reads standalone in print, HTML, and EPUB alike.

@heading The toolchain

The Kotlin edition is a Gradle multi-project build. The pins live once in
@file{kotlin/gradle/libs.versions.toml}, and every module builds against
them:

@multitable @columnfractions .40 .55
@item Kotlin (JVM, the K2 compiler) @tab 2.4.20
@item JDK, via @code{jvmToolchain(25)} @tab 25
@item Gradle @tab 9.7.1
@item Arrow (@code{Either}, @code{Raise}) @tab 2.2.3
@item kotlinx.collections.immutable @tab 0.5.2
@item Kotest (runner, assertions, property) @tab 6.2.5
@item ktlint engine @tab 1.8.0
@end multitable

@noindent
Two compiler settings are house rules, not suggestions. @code{allWarningsAsErrors}
turns every compiler warning into a build failure, and the lint gate is
@command{ktlint} 1.8.0 over every source set. The runtime module also
opts into @code{explicitApi()}, so its public declarations state their
visibility and return types in full.

@heading The layout

The @file{kotlin/} tree holds one @code{runtime} module and six chapter
modules, @code{ch0} through @code{ch5}. The runtime module is the package
@code{sicp.runtime}: the value type @code{Value}, the memoized stream type
@code{LStream}, the operation registry @code{OpTable}, the register machine
of chapter 5, the seeded random generator, and the typed data errors
@code{DatumError} and @code{DataError} that a fallible data operation
raises: @code{DatumError} for the @code{Datum} API of @ref{0.5},
@code{DataError} for the legacy @code{Value} helpers. Chapters 1 to 3 host
their programs in plain Kotlin; the interpreters of chapters 4 and 5 run
on the runtime types.

Each chapter module wires three source sets beyond @code{main}, and each
of the three reads one top-level directory:

@multitable @columnfractions .20 .32 .48
@item Source set @tab Directory @tab Contents
@item @code{examples} @tab @file{kotlin/examples/ch@var{N}/} @tab one file per book listing, @code{S@var{N}_@var{M}<Name>.kt}, with Kotest tests
@item @code{exercises} @tab @file{kotlin/exercises/ch@var{N}/} @tab one scaffold per exercise, @code{E@var{N}_@var{MM}.kt}, statement as KDoc
@item @code{solutions} @tab @file{kotlin/solutions/ch@var{N}/} @tab the same file names as @file{exercises/}, solved and tested
@end multitable

@noindent
Exercises and solutions share fully qualified names, and a test run receives
exactly one of the two on its classpath (@ref{0.7} shows the tasks that
choose). Chapter 0 itself is the primer you are reading; its module also
carries the three directories, and @ref{0.2} through @ref{0.7} use them as
they teach.

@heading The recipes

The @command{just} recipes wrap the Gradle tasks worth remembering:

@example
just setup      # ./gradlew --version: provision the JDK 25 toolchain
just fmt        # ./gradlew ktlintCheck
just lint       # ./gradlew build: the full gate
just test       # ./gradlew test examplesTest solutionsTest
just scaffold   # ./gradlew exercisesTest --continue: the pending report
@end example

@noindent
@command{just lint} is the gate: it compiles the runtime and the four
source sets of every chapter, applies ktlint, and runs the example and
solution tests. A scaffold that is still pending does not break it, which is
what @code{PendingSolution} is for.

@heading How a test proves an exercise

Every exercise in this book carries three artifacts. The statement lives in
the section text. The scaffold lives in @file{exercises/}: a function body
that throws @code{PendingSolution} and a test disabled with the reason
@code{"pending solution"}, so the scaffold compiles and the gate stays green
while it is unsolved. The solution lives in @file{solutions/} under the same
name: a real implementation whose test asserts exactly the values the book's
statement leads to. A listing file proves itself the same way, which the
shortest example in the book shows in full:

@example kotlin
package sicp.ch0.examples

import io.kotest.core.spec.style.FunSpec
import io.kotest.matchers.shouldBe

/** The section 0.1 tour: `square`. */
public fun tourSquare(x: Long): Long = x * x

public class S0_1TourTest :
    FunSpec(@{
        test("the call returns the value the prose annotates") @{
            tourSquare(21) shouldBe 441L
        @}
    @})
@end example

@noindent
The definition is the listing; the test is the proof. The file is
@file{kotlin/examples/ch0/S0_1Tour.kt}, and its call reads
@code{tourSquare(21)} with the result written beside it:

@example kotlin
tourSquare(21)
// => 441
@end example

@noindent
That comment shape is the book's interaction convention, introduced for real
in @ref{0.2} and specified in @ref{0.8}.

